Pre-parse <future> and <forward_list> in chromium's PCH to dodge the
mingw __try/__catch macro mismatch.

The mingw toolchain patch installs an override in build_config.h:

  #undef __try   ;   #define __try try
  #undef __except;   #define __except(x) catch(...)

(0029 extends that with the matching __catch / __throw_exception_again,
which keeps libstdc++ headers that use the __try/__catch macro pair
consistent.)

Some libstdc++ headers, however, mix the patterns. <future> writes:

      __try { ... }
  #if __cpp_exceptions
      catch (const system_error& __e) { ... }
  #endif

i.e. `__try` (a macro that goes through chromium's override and becomes
the real `try` keyword), and then a real `catch` clause that is
compiled out under -fno-exceptions. The result for clang+mingw is

  try { ... }  /* no catch */

which fails to parse:

  c++/16.1.0/future:1831: error: expected catch

This pattern is unique to <future> (basic_string.tcc, alloc_traits.h,
cow_string.h, exception_ptr.h all guard the `try` together with the
`catch`). The 0029 macro extension cannot help because the `catch`
clause in <future> is real C++, not a macro.

The chromium precompile.h already pre-parses a list of stdlib headers.
Add <future> there so the std::async template body is parsed once,
before any chromium TU has had a chance to redefine __try. <forward_list>
is added alongside for symmetry -- the 0029 fix already handles its
macro pair, but pre-parsing makes it independent of the override
ordering.

--- a/src/3rdparty/chromium/build/precompile.h
+++ b/src/3rdparty/chromium/build/precompile.h
@@ -42,8 +42,10 @@
 #include <cstdio>
 #include <cstdlib>
 #include <cstring>
+#include <forward_list>
 #include <fstream>
 #include <functional>
+#include <future>
 #include <iomanip>
 #include <iosfwd>
 #include <iterator>
